created: 2026-07-24, last updated: 2026-07-25, [Read more](https://fetchsandbox.com/blog/stripe-webhook-signature-raw-body-express)

Stripe signs the **raw** request body.
If your app parses the body before verification, the bytes your handler sees are not the bytes Stripe signed.
...
app.use(express.json());
...
`express.json()` already consumed and parsed the body.
By the time `constructEvent` runs, `req.body` is a parsed object, not the original bytes.
The signature was computed against the bytes, so verification fails — even though the request is legitimate.
...
The webhook route needs a raw body parser, mounted **before** the global JSON middleware sees it:
```
app.post(
"/webhooks/stripe",
express.raw({ type: "application/json" }),
(req, res) => {
const sig = req.headers["stripe-signature"];
const event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(req.body, sig, webhookSecret);
res.sendStatus(200);
}
);
// Global JSON parser for the rest of the app
app.use(express.json());
```
Order matters.
If you call `app.use(express.json())` first, it claims the body for every `application/json` request, including this one, and the raw bytes are lost.

